lima/ppir: dot2 with nothing to add is sum2add with a zero addend

reference UI's rounded rect (capture prog 3, 181 draws a frame on the Games screen)
computes length(max(q,0.0)).  The Mali blob does the square, the horizontal sum
and the square root in ONE instruction, @8 of capture/games/compare-26.2.6/
prog-3.blob.pp.txt:

  mul.v0 $0.xy $0.zwww $0.zwww, max.s0 $0.z $0.x $0.y,
  add.s1 $0.w $0.x $0.y, sqrt.s2 $0.w $0.w

lima needed three.  lima_nir_lower_fdot() turns fdot2 into fsum2(fmul) and then
lowers every fsum2 that no fadd consumes into a scalar fadd of the product's two
components; nir_opt_algebraic fuses the .x half of that fadd back into an fmad,
which recomputes the first product in the float multiply unit so the float
accumulator can add it to the second.  The result is a vec4 multiply of the pair
whose .x result is then dead, a scalar multiply that duplicates it, and the add:

  mul.v0 $0.zw $0.xxxy $0.xxxy
  mul.s0 $0.x $0.x, add.s1 $1.z ^s0 $0.w, sqrt.s2 $1.w $1.z

The vec4 accumulator's op 0x12 (sum2add, arg0.x + arg0.y + arg1.x; the
accumulator opcode mapper at 0x000e56e0 in libGLES_mali.so emits it for exactly
this horizontal sum) does the whole sum with arg1 = 0.0, reading the product
straight out of ^vmul, so the product, the sum and the root are one instruction:

  mul.v0 $0.xyxx $0.xyxx, sum2add.v1 $0.z ^v0.xyyy ^const0.xxxx,
  sqrt.s2 $0.w $0.z, const0 0.000000 ...

lima_nir_fsum2_is_sum2add() in ir/lima_ir.h is the shape test, shared by
lima_nir_lower_fdot()'s "lower what ppir cannot fuse" rule and by ppir's emit,
so the pass and the backend cannot disagree about which fsum2 survives.

Counts (one limacompile process per program, PP instructions):
  reference UI rounded rect, Games/Clock prog 3   14 -> 13 (blob 12 total, ~9 executed)
  reference UI rounded rect, Photos prog 6        14 -> 13
  reference UI textured rounded rect, Home prog 12  13 -> 12
  Games total 22 -> 21, Photos 16 -> 15, Clock 22 -> 21, Home 35 -> 33
  Demo unchanged (44), all GP counts unchanged.

Predicted GPU time: Games draws the rounded rect 181 times a frame over about
2.01 M covered pixels, so one instruction a pixel is 2.01 M instructions, about
1.7 ms of PP a frame on the Games screen; Home about 0.9 ms (prog 3 and prog 12
together), Clock about 0.5 ms, Photos about 0.1 ms, the Demo 0.

Picture risk: BIT-IDENTICAL.  Both forms multiply the same two pairs of operands
at the same precision, round each product into a pipeline register, and add the
two products once.  arg1 = +0.0 cannot change the result: the infinitely precise
value of (a + b + 0) is the value of (a + b), so whether the accumulator rounds
that sum once or twice it lands on the same number, and a + b here is a sum of
two squares, so the -0 + 0 = +0 case cannot arise.  Shots must be byte-identical
to the 0001-0015 build; any difference is a bug in the swizzle of arg0.

Depends on one hardware behaviour: the result combiner reads the register the
vec4 accumulator wrote in the SAME instruction (sqrt.s2 $0.w $0.z above).  lima
already relies on that for the FLOAT accumulator - the shipped Games prog 6 ends
with "add.sat.s1 $1.z ^s0 ^const0.x, mul.s2 $0.w ^uniform.w $1.zzzz" and renders
correctly - and the blob's @8 above does the same, but no captured program, blob
or lima, does it from the VEC4 accumulator.  The two accumulators are the same
pipeline stage, so this should hold; the forwarding test (case D of
a separate finite arithmetic test) checks it on the board, and
if it fails the saving disappears (the rounded rect goes back to 14) rather than
turning into a wrong picture, because ppir would simply put the sqrt in the next
instruction.

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/lima_ir.h b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/lima_ir.h
index 4538946..0a96054 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/lima_ir.h
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/lima_ir.h
@@ -48,6 +48,59 @@
    fprintf(stderr, "ppir: " __VA_ARGS__)
 
 
+/* True when ppir turns this fsum2 into one vec4-accumulator sum2add (op 0x12,
+ * arg0.x + arg0.y + arg1.x): its two components have to come from a
+ * two-component fmul in the same block that nothing else reads, so the product
+ * can be read straight out of ^vmul.
+ *
+ * Shared by lima_nir_lower_fdot()'s "lower the fsum2 ppir cannot fuse" rule and
+ * by ppir's nir->ppir emit, so the two cannot disagree: if the rule lowered an
+ * fsum2 that ppir could have fused the shader gets an extra instruction, and if
+ * it kept one ppir cannot fuse it gets two.
+ */
+static inline bool
+lima_nir_fsum2_is_sum2add(const nir_alu_instr *fsum2)
+{
+   if (fsum2->op != nir_op_fsum2 || fsum2->def.num_components != 1)
+      return false;
+
+   /* arg0 reads components 0 and 1 of the product, in that order */
+   if (fsum2->src[0].swizzle[0] != 0 || fsum2->src[0].swizzle[1] != 1)
+      return false;
+
+   nir_instr *parent = nir_def_instr(fsum2->src[0].src.ssa);
+   if (parent->type != nir_instr_type_alu || parent->block != fsum2->instr.block)
+      return false;
+
+   const nir_alu_instr *fmul = nir_instr_as_alu(parent);
+
+   return fmul->op == nir_op_fmul && fmul->def.num_components == 2 &&
+          list_is_singular(&fmul->def.uses);
+}
+
+/* nir_algebraic condition: lower this fsum2 into a scalar fadd of its two
+ * components, because ppir can fuse neither shape of sum2add out of it. */
+static inline bool
+lima_nir_fsum2_must_lower(const nir_alu_instr *instr)
+{
+   /* every use an fadd: ppir fuses the fsum2 into it, that fadd's other
+    * source becoming sum2add's arg1 */
+   bool only_fadd = true;
+   nir_foreach_use(use, &instr->def) {
+      const nir_instr *user = nir_src_use_instr(use);
+      if (user->type != nir_instr_type_alu ||
+          nir_instr_as_alu(user)->op != nir_op_fadd) {
+         only_fadd = false;
+         break;
+      }
+   }
+   if (only_fadd)
+      return false;
+
+   /* no such fadd, but sum2add with arg1 = 0.0 is still one instruction */
+   return !lima_nir_fsum2_is_sum2add(instr);
+}
+
 struct ra_regs;
 struct lima_vs_compiled_shader;
 struct lima_fs_compiled_shader;
diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/lima_nir_algebraic.py b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/lima_nir_algebraic.py
index 7d0336b..744c458 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/lima_nir_algebraic.py
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/lima_nir_algebraic.py
@@ -42,9 +42,13 @@ lower_fdot = [
    (('fdot2', 'a', 'b'), ('fsum2', ('fmul', 'a', 'b'))),
    (('fdot3', 'a', 'b'), ('fsum3', ('fmul', 'a', 'b'))),
    (('fdot4', 'a', 'b'), ('fsum4', ('fmul', 'a', 'b'))),
-   # An fsum2 whose only users are fadds stays: ppir fuses fadd(fsum2(a), b)
-   # into the vec4 accumulator's sum2add (op 0x12), one instruction.
-   (('fsum2(is_not_only_used_by_fadd)', 'a'), ('fadd', 'a.x', 'a.y')),
+   # An fsum2 ppir can fuse into the vec4 accumulator's sum2add (op 0x12) stays
+   # one instruction, so it is not lowered: either every user is an fadd, whose
+   # other source becomes sum2add's arg1, or the two components come out of a
+   # two-component fmul ppir reads from ^vmul and arg1 is 0.0.
+   # lima_nir_fsum2_must_lower() (ir/lima_ir.h) is the same test ppir's emit
+   # makes, so the pass and the backend cannot disagree.
+   (('fsum2(lima_nir_fsum2_must_lower)', 'a'), ('fadd', 'a.x', 'a.y')),
 ]
 
